- op: discuss  # 0400
  at: '2026-08-12T07:53:52Z'  # 0400
  by: agent  # 0400
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0400
  clock: 4  # 0400
  nonce: '0400'  # 0400
  message: "SATISFIED, by the same change that closed INV-buzab — and the two arms had to go\ntogether, because leaving either one standing leaves a permitting branch that\nanswers a question adjacent to \"did I look\".\n\nTHIS ITEM'S OWN REPRO WAS GO-ONLY AND REQUIRED A RESPELL. IT REPRODUCES ON\nPYTHON, ON THE SHIPPED CATALOGUE, WITH NO RESPELL, NO OVERLAY AND NO THIRD-PARTY\nDEPENDENCY — which is a stronger statement of the same defect and is what the\nregression tests now pin:\n\n    # main.py\n    import os\n    def persist(data):\n        fd = os.open(\"/etc/evil.conf\", os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT)\n        os.write(fd, data)\n        os.close(fd)\n\n    claim fs_write / must_not_exist  ->  BEFORE: confirmed, rc 0\n                                          AFTER: inconclusive, rc 2\n\n    POSITIVE CONTROL, same claim, catalogued primitives instead:\n    os.makedirs(p); os.remove(p)     ->  violated, 2 chains, rc 1 in BOTH arms.\n\n`os` carries 40 rows. None of them is `os.open`, `os.write`, `os.read`,\n`os.truncate` or `os.sendfile`. Presence of the 40 vouched for the rest. The\nnetwork version of the same shape (`os.sendfile(sock_fd, file_fd, 0, 4096)`,\nclaim net_send) also confirmed and is also now inconclusive.\n\nTHE ITEM UNDER-STATED ITS OWN SCOPE IN ONE RESPECT: the presence test is\nBOUNDARY-blind as well as surface-blind. `catalog.primitives` is flat across all\n15 boundary types, so a module catalogued only for the filesystem vouched for a\nNETWORK claim — on the shipped python catalogue that is `csv`, `pickle`,\n`shelve`, `dbm`, `fileinput` and `importlib.resources`. Same fix closes it.\n\nTHE THREE CANDIDATE DIRECTIONS THIS ITEM FILED, adjudicated:\n\n (a) a per-catalogue `adjudicable_modules:` list — NOT BUILT. It is (b) with a\n     new field name, and a new field would have to be defined, validated,\n     documented and axis-declared while `stdlib_module_completeness` already\n     exists with per-module granularity, a required `retrieved:` provenance\n     date, load-time validation that rejects a completeness claim without one,\n     and a hand-authored-only contract stated in `scripts/refresh-stdlib-modules`\n     (\"closed-world claims demand per-module human audit, not a script that\n     scrapes interpreter state\"). Adding a second home for one fact is the\n     failure mode this project's own rules name.\n\n (b) require a completeness signal per module — BUILT, but ONLY as the second\n     of two branches, and the first one is what carries the load. Five\n     adversarial reviewers refuted the version that made completeness the\n     sole permit: a module-level answer called `os` unexamined while the\n     same run classified os.mkdir through it, and a json.dump fixture\n     printed \"no I/O catalog coverage (builtins, json)\" one line above\n     \"2 fs_write chain(s) found\" THROUGH those modules. THE UNIT OF\n     EXAMINATION IS THE CALL SITE. Shipped rule: (1) the catalogue\n     CLASSIFIED this call -> examined; (2) else the module carries a dated\n     closed-world audit -> examined negative; (3) else first-party; (4)\n     else block. This item's own repro is closed by (1)+(2) together:\n     os.open/os.write are unclassified AND `os` is unaudited.\n\n (c) \"make the two module-matching rules ONE predicate\" — DELIBERATELY NOT DONE,\n     and this is the one place I disagree with the item as filed. The tagger's\n     `_module_matches` is component-suffix-aware and PERMISSIVE; the gate must be\n     strict. Unifying toward the tagger would make MORE modules adjudicable and\n     manufacture MORE false confirms — the short `unix` spelling this item's own\n     measurement showed to be protective is protective precisely because the two\n     rules differ. The rules are now separated ON PURPOSE, each documents why it\n     is calibrated the way it is, and the gate's rule is pinned by cases:\n     EXACT — not prefix, not suffix, not component. A prefix draft was\n     refuted too: `urllib` is a namespace package with unrelated submodule\n     I/O surfaces, Go's `/` is not containment (`os` would have vouched for\n     `os/exec`), and Rust's `::` was not in the separator list at all.\n\n     The item's underlying worry — \"a future catalogue edit that normalises a\n     module string is a security-relevant change wearing the costume of a typo\n     fix\" — is closed anyway, and structurally rather than by vigilance: a\n     respell changes `p.module` on ROWS, and rows no longer grant coverage at\n     all. Only an explicit dated completeness declaration does, and you cannot\n     write one by accident while tidying a string. And an overlay cannot\n     write one at all — a six-line overlay with zero rows and one\n     completeness entry was measured turning the INV-buzab exfiltration\n     fixture from inconclusive rc 2 back to confirmed rc 0, so overlays now\n     raise on that field.\n\nCOST, measured, six repos / six languages, with production's own\n`_uncatalogued_external_modules` as the control: ZERO verdict changes. Every\nrepo with third-party dependencies was already inconclusive. See INV-buzab's\nthread for the table."  # 0400
